#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275259 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6861910f-6d1e-51d4-89aa-e94ccdc1daef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464408} {:answer/id #uuid "2ff3e9d8-9224-590f-a556-21e0f19a6cd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464409}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464408} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2d825a4-6901-57a2-9930-5d754820d8f2" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465138} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3073e19f-2806-5672-b534-cfe2db245ac2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464410} {:answer/id #uuid "090dcb5c-5958-5538-8b54-75141969de9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464411}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464411} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6c59f83-3272-51a6-b5bb-233286cf4fae" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465139} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f071eee-8c41-5a00-9649-943ecfb7e8f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464412} {:answer/id #uuid "0d3e4fef-b214-5bad-bd98-71272ba97893" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464413}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464412} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe47fa17-0302-5990-92cb-7f28cb8bce17" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465140}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5a5b0685-d096-5d41-9e82-0f5600340c35" :question/math-academy-id "q-150018" :question/problem "![](images/67/678ab825415e107bca9554d90e2ad5a4d8cc90a5ef68d817972ef98dfc165922.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ is true. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/0e/0e8d0fcf619987f49869cc6ae9eb5cb0c7acbbb23f45ce8f64626dfd1debdc63.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/b2/b2bec1f1da172b623f20d28fd0e9c5a248cf00dc621769851ca33b3b545b8592.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275403 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d8f1b2b-173b-5b92-b859-1ec4e15fc869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272505} {:answer/id #uuid "2e0ec060-43c6-5507-a4b4-71bf27bfdb41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272798} {:answer/id #uuid "3d24278c-1604-5a0e-92c8-91b968fed491" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272912} {:answer/id #uuid "6793780e-4123-58f7-8dac-1bc0e63caa25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273279} {:answer/id #uuid "7c9ffd0c-5022-5c83-b63e-3c3cbf9ed38c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273455}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272798} :answer-field/id #uuid "30cc30d9-eb2f-5ad6-9d67-21da1b5948c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274721}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3ccbcc93-cb2d-5c7d-ae5c-a7e728fdad2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-15792" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{5} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278183 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "044aa764-a402-587b-9d4c-b41637dfa030" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275516} {:answer/id #uuid "8324a325-41e1-54bc-8b74-1c52b4799e1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276657} {:answer/id #uuid "c4127d31-6dc3-521f-8215-809b40a67775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277220} {:answer/id #uuid "d2c6cd3a-dd09-5f85-a646-0e12c483a833" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277337} {:answer/id #uuid "e2c3aad5-29e3-5b96-b737-92612b8b8109" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277461}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277461} :answer-field/id #uuid "17b8f849-09df-52e8-a517-f7b406de368f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "48febb37-71be-5629-b005-ff375ac1902f" :question/math-academy-id "q-15843" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{6} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278243 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cbcfb89-b518-52f4-a378-bc4f54d231b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "xy{e}^{{x}^{2}y}-\\frac{2y}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275879} {:answer/id #uuid "32c61380-4849-507d-b3b3-d576421fd9c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{{x}^{2}y}-\\frac{y}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275927} {:answer/id #uuid "3e7cffcb-bc87-530f-a986-8f950f2b921b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x{e}^{{x}^{2}y}-\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276019} {:answer/id #uuid "8277d213-c7d3-506d-a625-8ce16ed02b84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2xy{e}^{{x}^{2}y}-\\frac{y}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276653} {:answer/id #uuid "ca03764d-edf4-532a-81b1-cb8796cca4c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2y{e}^{{x}^{2}y}-\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277273}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276653} :answer-field/id #uuid "891d87b2-b404-5b6e-a0fe-b586955b1e92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277969}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "424001c9-342e-51ae-ba3f-40228db99e48" :question/math-academy-id "q-159656" :question/problem "Find ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ for $f(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}y}-y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{{x}^{2}y}-y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{{x}^{2}y})-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the chain and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{{x}^{2}y})-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) & ={e}^{{x}^{2}y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y)-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{2}y}\\cdot y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})-y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{2}y}\\cdot y\\cdot 2x-y\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =2xy{e}^{{x}^{2}y}-\\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278244 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4008d421-bcc4-541e-b6d1-6f911e6c8d66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1})}{2x\\sqrt{2xy-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276034} {:answer/id #uuid "430b88eb-d781-5955-bdd4-cba096425762" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1})}{\\sqrt{2xy-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276071} {:answer/id #uuid "4fccdda9-5259-5b0d-9374-c4a4d5a9b18d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1})}{\\sqrt{2xy-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276193} {:answer/id #uuid "ba43033d-0504-5559-9fb4-0a81d932ee52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1})}{\\sqrt{2xy-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277130} {:answer/id #uuid "f142f4cb-083a-5c32-9524-ba93f1707e6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1})}{\\sqrt{2xy-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277595}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276071} :answer-field/id #uuid "05705cd0-c421-54fa-8117-fe18fa6f3bc3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0833bc78-75ed-516b-b7f3-7df11e162117" :question/math-academy-id "q-159658" :question/problem "Find ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ for $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1}).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the chain rule: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1})) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{2xy-1}) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2xy-1}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the sum and the constant multiple rules for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2xy-1}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-1) & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1})}{2\\sqrt{2xy-1}}(2x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1})}{2\\sqrt{2xy-1}}\\cdot (2x\\cdot 1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1})}{\\sqrt{2xy-1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278245 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28d0f78d-518d-58c8-8c0b-81173c0184aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275847} {:answer/id #uuid "87e360c8-9939-5436-951b-6294befe5b85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276706} {:answer/id #uuid "b2eb3a7e-c309-596e-bc28-cb1b69bf6690" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277079} {:answer/id #uuid "b5e642e7-633d-5cd9-a804-aa02d2102aa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277097} {:answer/id #uuid "e08c2618-3181-5d40-a286-0a8436c4dcbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277439}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277439} :answer-field/id #uuid "dde4342f-9533-561d-aa12-ff6ee3bf5a50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2942efcf-e503-5d5c-b252-e682ffa52f18" :question/math-academy-id "q-159660" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy),$ evaluate ${f}_{x}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},2).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the chain and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\cdot y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\cdot (2x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\cdot y\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =2x{y}^{2}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},2):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},2) & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot {2}^{2}+2\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =2\\pi +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\pi +2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278246 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "124ba60a-ec47-5dd9-97fc-d33ccf7d0e0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275645} {:answer/id #uuid "5aa03d48-f644-5066-b304-d3fc3f4f62eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276292} {:answer/id #uuid "5c6c70be-1878-50b5-989f-a8ed0e5f587f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276303} {:answer/id #uuid "a0b747a7-3b88-5c3b-b25b-53403f09d052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276925} {:answer/id #uuid "ec320ce3-f38d-5b16-8a80-a7b57185a46a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277549}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275645} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d4deb5e-7cf6-55ae-b93a-f09121cdee49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "95a36042-f58f-5fde-b6a7-7ca45f1d6809" :question/math-academy-id "q-159700" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x+y}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y},$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ at the point $(\\pi ,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{x+y}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}\\right) & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡y-(x+y)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)}{\\operatorname{sin}^{2}⁡y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡y-(x+y)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{\\operatorname{sin}^{2}⁡y} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-(x+y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{\\operatorname{sin}^{2}⁡y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(\\pi ,\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(\\pi ,\\pi /2)} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-(\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-0}{{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278247 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8543754a-baf7-532f-9aaa-3f6e2f377989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276670} {:answer/id #uuid "872098d7-2da8-563d-9dbe-aa0ef1581439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276693} {:answer/id #uuid "9f76ba1c-fbca-5e67-ba0f-95af73b7592a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276912} {:answer/id #uuid "f00a4060-bf7f-5ccd-83cc-feaade45b7ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277585} {:answer/id #uuid "f0139e5c-4ac7-5bda-a049-12fd07049209" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277586}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277585} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b4f281e-3538-5167-b8db-a9dda1fcb94e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277972}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8c6d2d99-f435-50ff-85c3-a966d3aa1af6" :question/math-academy-id "q-159709" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ at the point $(0,2).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{x}{y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\right) & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)\\cdot (y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{(y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{(y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{(y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(0,2):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(0,2)} & =\\frac{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-0\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{(2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+0}{(2+0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a420460-c7c1-505e-8ad4-c9bb108a6ff2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275860} {:answer/id #uuid "5f9d3b68-4176-5fa0-9407-0ed4c3eb41a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276332} {:answer/id #uuid "883a161b-2779-571d-bfa4-409b6a959feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4y-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276708} {:answer/id #uuid "b96f1c91-267d-539d-9fa8-52a5e328fc12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x+5y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277120} {:answer/id #uuid "dfef293c-ea14-5c29-b5c4-17120a6c650d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277434}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277120} :answer-field/id #uuid "af4b38e9-c4a5-5254-be14-837fef119079" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c75dffc7-c60f-5338-92f4-7c0c6ff2bb72" :question/math-academy-id "q-159713" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{3x+y}{\\sqrt{x+y}}.$ The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{h(x,y)}{2(x+y)\\sqrt{x+y}}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the quotient and chain rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{3x+y}{\\sqrt{x+y}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x+y)\\cdot \\sqrt{x+y}-(3x+y)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{x+y})}{{(\\sqrt{x+y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3\\cdot \\sqrt{x+y}-(3x+y)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y))}{{(\\sqrt{x+y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3\\cdot \\sqrt{x+y}-(3x+y)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y}}\\cdot 1)}{{(\\sqrt{x+y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{6(x+y)-(3x+y)}{2\\sqrt{x+y}}\\right)}{x+y} \\\\  & =\\frac{3x+5y}{2(x+y)\\sqrt{x+y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nh(x,y)=3x+5y.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278249 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1071740e-9558-5e73-b52c-d383864bf497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275622} {:answer/id #uuid "9e058e90-be98-5313-a164-e37b5eaec24f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-x{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276896} {:answer/id #uuid "b028380b-a597-535a-bd93-8b4c6734b700" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277053} {:answer/id #uuid "bb6cb830-da03-564b-9a88-50ef70b7374f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3+{x}^{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277142} {:answer/id #uuid "e1d0d589-d5cc-550b-974e-b4afdfaebb22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277451}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276896} :answer-field/id #uuid "75024153-b4e7-530d-be37-6a2d7adf8ab7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ad571fe8-149e-54b6-a89f-66ae79db7481" :question/math-academy-id "q-159714" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2x{y}^{2}-1}{{x}^{2}y}.$ The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{2h(x,y)}{{x}^{3}y}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{2x{y}^{2}-1}{{x}^{2}y}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{y}^{2}-1)\\cdot {x}^{2}y-(2x{y}^{2}-1)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y)}{({x}^{2}y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{y}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}y-(2x{y}^{2}-1)\\cdot 2xy}{{x}^{4}{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2xy}{{x}^{4}{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2xy-2{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}{{x}^{4}{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2xy(1-x{y}^{2})}{{x}^{4}{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-x{y}^{2})}{{x}^{3}y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nh(x,y)=1-x{y}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278250 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05f76324-5a68-5230-a5cd-bf08feffd775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4{x}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275530} {:answer/id #uuid "89f30304-64a1-56ba-bd73-bcc48e23b10a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276724} {:answer/id #uuid "9562c48d-8b5b-53a4-9421-210d904f36b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{x}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276823} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ad9708-221f-5829-9e8f-6b54521f34fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{x}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276941} {:answer/id #uuid "dd43b247-ee16-5db2-b7b2-06fdc79123a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277415}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276941} :answer-field/id #uuid "d372165d-773c-522b-a6ae-4635a798bcf6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7c4ebeea-f58f-5eb8-93aa-37b6d0eeb259" :question/math-academy-id "q-159716" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ for $f(x,y)={x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x{y}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x{y}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-{y}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-{y}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) & =(\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4})\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{x}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x))-{y}^{3}\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =((4{x}^{3})\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{x}^{4}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x))-{y}^{3} \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{x}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278251 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69e44334-00de-5702-83e5-16a7570d043c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x(2x+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276427} {:answer/id #uuid "8aac973e-8257-545d-b095-ab149936e003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276733} {:answer/id #uuid "b7cf2fa9-6053-5c4d-852e-474c37939e76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2xy(1-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277108} {:answer/id #uuid "dd338f07-9cf2-51fd-a11c-32c271645616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2xy(1+x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277414} {:answer/id #uuid "f9b315c7-d154-5db9-8a29-731dc66826e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-x{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277662}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277414} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c3c66f0-c7ef-59da-9462-dd34d39d1caf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "485c686b-8a53-5bf0-8bc4-72a915a1ba56" :question/math-academy-id "q-159723" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{x-{y}^{2}}.$ The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{h(x,y)}{{(x-{y}^{2})}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{x-{y}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot (x-{y}^{2})-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-{y}^{2})}{{(x-{y}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y\\cdot (x-{y}^{2})-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot (-2y)}{{(x-{y}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2xy-2{y}^{3}+2{x}^{2}y+2{y}^{3}}{{(x-{y}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2xy+2{x}^{2}y}{{(x-{y}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2xy(1+x)}{{(x-{y}^{2})}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nh(x,y)=2xy(1+x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278254 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47d515c6-7925-5320-a23c-240a300aec93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276111} {:answer/id #uuid "5d686b90-3df8-52b1-80bd-25c137cc92e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276311} {:answer/id #uuid "60c10891-1d7b-5e75-a24f-b405299f1c94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276345} {:answer/id #uuid "90b73919-9597-5b2d-970e-88b31b287e29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276790} {:answer/id #uuid "e26f7a61-188b-57c0-b352-c69a4a8d07d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277459}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277459} :answer-field/id #uuid "b11e4e9d-db09-5e46-8d26-27def32836dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278034}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fd55c472-88b5-5201-8165-4111e38766b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-159752" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+x\\sqrt{y},$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{4},4).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+x\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))+\\sqrt{y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the product, the chain, and the constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))+\\sqrt{y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) & =(\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)))+\\sqrt{y}\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =(1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+x\\cdot \\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy))+\\sqrt{y} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+\\frac{1}{y}\\cdot y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x))+\\sqrt{y} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+1+\\sqrt{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(\\frac{1}{4},4):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(1/4,4)} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4)+1+\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+1+2 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278259 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "279e76f9-f9f0-5564-ae7c-148de9a68761" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275840} {:answer/id #uuid "4db3fc28-5e05-5ec0-9cc6-98a0f4ab9c44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276163} {:answer/id #uuid "55ba531b-b121-55b8-9aa2-75e80c2df8e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276239} {:answer/id #uuid "9ad34d84-aa0e-5791-b041-c5ef3f6d8bab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276864} {:answer/id #uuid "f1d1bcdc-b7ea-5e71-b840-58edcde3907f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277602}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276239} :answer-field/id #uuid "57d1b1f3-769f-55bc-82da-be45d6d73f43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277882}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3f9cc492-b3ba-556f-a019-182cbc410bcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-159766" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},$ evaluate ${f}_{y}(2,\\sqrt{5}).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)\\cdot \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain, the sum, and the constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)\\cdot \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) & =1\\cdot \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+y\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot ({x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot ({x}^{2}\\cdot 0+2y) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(2,\\sqrt{5}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y}{|}_{(2,\\sqrt{5})} & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{5})}^{2}}+\\frac{{(\\sqrt{5})}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{5})}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+5}+\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{4+5}} \\\\  & =3+\\frac{5}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278260 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a78e326-4c51-50ac-aad3-5c443d334667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275864} {:answer/id #uuid "5bbfe750-2540-5243-bc59-e0f79dc31902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276300} {:answer/id #uuid "6a035055-68f4-5be4-adae-4803c1279845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276429} {:answer/id #uuid "8b99f649-3f43-5a3f-8b56-e0ddedb38c57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276746} {:answer/id #uuid "9ca8f984-ab7a-5d4f-91f6-049dec25f1e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276884}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275864} :answer-field/id #uuid "f21cf5ea-df9a-5241-8868-5373b87209ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278150}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e7a86e6a-207f-552d-b9da-b1e5c673da4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-159768" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ at the point $(2,\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) & =x(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y))+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\  & =x(1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y))+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\  & =x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(2,\\pi ):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y}{|}_{(2,\\pi )} & =2\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =2(-1-\\pi \\cdot 0)+(-1) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278287 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "543c2b9c-325b-52f0-b396-90565c46c411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u+2v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276224} {:answer/id #uuid "92c19161-8314-50f9-8577-fa4d8021b843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276806} {:answer/id #uuid "d2669ec8-53ac-5fd9-8ed2-7a7d577e4da4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277334} {:answer/id #uuid "f5091b71-1261-5b43-99a4-254b54528a4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277623} {:answer/id #uuid "f7c8bae6-1f2b-5a5c-b655-a34f3416c74e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3u-v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277643}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277334} :answer-field/id #uuid "810cd0e6-84ff-58ed-b739-3c8c64751fe5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277951}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "680f7a12-0c16-5883-aa06-5376f25863fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-160381" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3u+2v \\\\ u+3v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=3u+2v$ and $y(u,v)=u+3v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =9-2 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278288 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27184dd5-9306-5151-ba71-71c0184555b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275833} {:answer/id #uuid "921684d8-1a85-53da-b8a7-23bd356d4d8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276799} {:answer/id #uuid "a6b6ecd9-7efa-5fbf-99f4-87cf3cf2fc2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276980} {:answer/id #uuid "a9f4d180-9148-56f8-af89-c87393729376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277007} {:answer/id #uuid "bc4ef6cf-c02f-531a-934f-84c5afac9290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277153}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276799} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f823688-c59b-5a64-9e51-9b7c368087e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277821}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2b1f8f7b-fd82-5c84-a276-7fa2fd9b071a" :question/math-academy-id "q-160383" :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}uv \\\\ \\frac{v}{u}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x=uv$ and $y=\\frac{v}{u}.$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{u}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}v & u \\\\ -\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{1}{u}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =v\\cdot \\frac{1}{u}\\text{ }-u\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2v}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-2,3)}=\\frac{2(3)}{-2}=-3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278291 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b2ac211-6ede-500a-9363-0c90ed1326ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275729} {:answer/id #uuid "241de261-1031-55ca-a058-00911568c512" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275796} {:answer/id #uuid "54f35b13-d77e-5a66-b2b8-4f7e13bf0284" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276233} {:answer/id #uuid "b3da8c48-0f97-556c-90bd-47b71461d13c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277086} {:answer/id #uuid "c49fcad0-5cc2-5449-8be5-a58c6c177d96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277224}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275729} :answer-field/id #uuid "795a7cef-1005-511a-9200-2ed1ac91ad82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2443aa6d-e746-5170-93b3-e30684a9a33b" :question/math-academy-id "q-160402" :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{u-v} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(uv)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={e}^{u-v}$ and $y(u,v)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(uv).$  \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u-v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-{e}^{u-v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =\\frac{1}{u} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{v}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{u-v} & -{e}^{u-v} \\\\ \\frac{1}{u} & \\frac{1}{v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{u-v}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{v}\\right)-(-{e}^{u-v})\\cdot \\frac{1}{u} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{u-v}}{v}+\\frac{{e}^{u-v}}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(1,1)}=\\frac{{e}^{(1)-(1)}}{(1)}+\\frac{{e}^{(1)-(1)}}{(1)}=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278292 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c4a3210-10ef-5b25-901e-ab593d9722a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275737} {:answer/id #uuid "97420ee7-fdf5-5150-958a-35de289cfc75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{u+v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276836} {:answer/id #uuid "e66d3a94-2dd5-54c7-b4bb-4f280703014c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277489} {:answer/id #uuid "fd4e4f14-3265-58a7-acfe-48a134dd8de1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{u+v}(u-v)}{vu}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277697} {:answer/id #uuid "fd6c8ca7-ffb6-520e-b5af-e3963f09a153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{u+v}}{vu}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277699}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277697} :answer-field/id #uuid "beae5e95-9df5-5eed-ba44-7362dc1d3cb1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9c12a73a-3560-5ca0-8c93-5fcc98faad4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-160403" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $$\n\\displaystyle T(\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{u+v} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(uv)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={e}^{u+v}$ and $y(u,v)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(uv).$  \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u+v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & ={e}^{u+v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =\\frac{1}{u} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{v}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{u+v} & {e}^{u+v} \\\\ \\frac{1}{u} & \\frac{1}{v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{v}\\right)-{e}^{u+v}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{u}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{u+v}}{v}-\\frac{{e}^{u+v}}{u} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{u+v}(u-v)}{vu}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278293 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4de50ced-13d2-5b52-b330-c08b4accea21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276167} {:answer/id #uuid "7915ec34-4814-56e1-8467-fefea7d9c01e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276575} {:answer/id #uuid "cbaa62e7-62ed-563f-ad65-988b473507fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277288} {:answer/id #uuid "e6764292-1e5d-59b1-9254-fa0803749a91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277490} {:answer/id #uuid "fa1ea9dc-6100-51cd-aced-9070d203b9cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277665}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277665} :answer-field/id #uuid "41b727bf-5aef-5fca-a0c0-b0c3271d7bf9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "18d1f220-8bd2-586b-90ca-87751084600f" :question/math-academy-id "q-160405" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $$\n\\displaystyle T(\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) \\\\ 4u+6v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)$ and $y=4u+6v.$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =4 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) \\\\ \\,\\text{ }4\\, & \\,\\text{ }6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)\\cdot 6-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v))\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278296 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a030248-80e5-5392-828d-2bf5b0f34c56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275988} {:answer/id #uuid "46441063-cd17-57f8-93da-47999b8389f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276099} {:answer/id #uuid "4d076db5-f0b8-5a55-b9f9-a751427c3ef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276158} {:answer/id #uuid "8d6588ee-c55c-5f42-9ca1-f5f8f0e473ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276758} {:answer/id #uuid "dc0b5777-e3d4-5733-95a0-9024964404d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277404}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275988} :answer-field/id #uuid "ade1226d-3be4-5422-9ead-d085939320b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278025}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "861c1236-2662-5b7d-b150-03b6774d0f17" :question/math-academy-id "q-160427" :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} \\\\ {u}^{2}-{v}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 2v \\\\ 2u & -2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-2v)-2v\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =-4uv-4uv \\\\  & =-8uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-2,1)}=-8(-2)(1)=16.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278297 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7338df5d-9ede-525b-86f7-9575217f0621" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276521} {:answer/id #uuid "9194b3db-7eb6-5b12-82d1-c39ced725329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276796} {:answer/id #uuid "9f339d05-8ec4-52fc-929d-9e259b65d946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276908} {:answer/id #uuid "ae707e98-98f8-52a3-8963-4e1ec7a26492" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277042} {:answer/id #uuid "e115090f-5258-55ab-a7de-95ef1c7a3b91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277445}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276521} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc930da6-f008-59fd-875c-d797cf29d182" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278084}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "19415143-780a-5182-8164-c1af6838225c" :question/math-academy-id "q-160428" :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}}{2} \\\\ uv\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=\\frac{{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}}{2}$ and $y(u,v)=uv.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}u & -v \\\\ v & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =u\\cdot u-(-v)\\cdot v \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-1,-2)}=(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278298 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09e0e11f-ca64-573d-aa4a-39c0085748ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275562} {:answer/id #uuid "2571ec42-1410-5512-abb4-5d9f33e67cf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275817} {:answer/id #uuid "6056f233-baaf-59fc-ab26-47501271c259" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276339} {:answer/id #uuid "e97346e5-01ac-55ce-b840-edd902ee3c65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277517} {:answer/id #uuid "fa81b135-dfdc-52eb-85bc-1dd61c22d3d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277669}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277517} :answer-field/id #uuid "946d36e5-48fa-5671-b5c2-6b9df0953fbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277988}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "876a5d15-9cbd-5150-8246-c71dbabaa45c" :question/math-academy-id "q-160431" :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{v}^{2}+uv \\\\ v-u\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={v}^{2}+uv$ and $y(u,v)=v-u.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2v+u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}v & 2v+u \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =v\\cdot 1-(2v+u)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =v+2v+u \\\\  & =3v+u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(8,-2)}=3(-2)+8=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278300 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "201b5adb-fe9e-56a4-a103-cfa7fd40fca9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275775} {:answer/id #uuid "6b7b2609-013a-5fdd-ad02-4411dd975fa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276441} {:answer/id #uuid "7c015757-c6cd-5661-8772-840f2ddff2cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276607} {:answer/id #uuid "803b5ba9-187d-5e4c-a266-d33068d508bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276640} {:answer/id #uuid "af79779a-7d08-51aa-a7a1-413703ca604d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277048}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277048} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed0e005f-2ede-5db6-90a3-c44e813e721d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278140}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4f68c62f-9f11-5647-83b4-1238f13388b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-160440" :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\pi /6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$ and $y(u,v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡v-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\pi /6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(\\frac{\\pi }{6},0)}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278391 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "430ab58b-bfb3-5ff3-a26e-50e397241376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276070} {:answer/id #uuid "5ccc6906-339a-55eb-81c6-e1340f0e3809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276304} {:answer/id #uuid "982d058a-fa6a-596f-b4ad-7f3c34706a59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276844} {:answer/id #uuid "a9293427-c368-56c5-9cb1-7d5a9b4ac305" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277002} {:answer/id #uuid "c4de9476-c71d-52f0-8f43-c4631b831c7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277228}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276304} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a05d06b-9876-5842-ad9e-eec5778c2d3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277999}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "fb6920aa-28a6-5cb5-a583-f417d82bbe6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161127" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}{x}^{2}-2yz\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\nx=u+v,\\,y=u,\\,z=v\n$$\n\nfor $-1\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\,u,\\,v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨1,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+-{1}^{2}+-{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & ={x}^{2}-2yz\\,{|}_{x=u+v\\,y=u\\,z=v} \\\\  & =(u+v)^{2}-2uv \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}{x}^{2}-2yz\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,({u}^{2}+{v}^{2})\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}+u{v}^{2}]}_{u=-1}^{u=1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{3}+2{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{[\\frac{2}{3}v+\\frac{2}{3}{v}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{2}{3})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278393 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "407b054a-ad2a-5003-b975-08ebe629a652" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "({v}^{3}+2v)\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276038} {:answer/id #uuid "4c0afc67-2eaf-50ff-a740-16da7f2c7971" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "({u}^{3}+{v}^{3})\\sqrt{16{v}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276147} {:answer/id #uuid "533273b3-79e7-5ab4-8e9c-12dbf680ea3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "({v}^{2}+2u)\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276214} {:answer/id #uuid "6262307b-481e-5435-ad06-56dd718d0e29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(u+v)\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276366} {:answer/id #uuid "ae06b05f-02ef-5a1f-88b5-160bedb18ca3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "({u}^{3}+2v)\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277041}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277041} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e029e9e-eb95-5dc6-b173-809d27542530" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277920}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0739fb8b-67dd-5c96-8b51-b9fe22150a63" :question/math-academy-id "q-161132" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=xy+z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,{u}^{2},\\,2v⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}+4}$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}+4}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(xy+z)\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y={u}^{2}\\,z=2v} \\\\  & =u.{u}^{2}+2v \\\\  & ={u}^{3}+2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,({u}^{3}+2v)\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}+4}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278394 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40de9f5d-9bb8-505f-856c-ce2c6913769b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{8}\\,{u}^{3}{v}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276043} {:answer/id #uuid "9a30988f-5c27-5ce0-8684-641402fd6ea6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{8}\\,{u}^{4}{v}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276861} {:answer/id #uuid "c2412b0e-122f-55ad-9a36-81cdc271d082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{8}\\,{u}^{2}{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277204} {:answer/id #uuid "d49654d0-756c-5b91-9e96-98d9917a2ace" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{8}\\,{u}^{3}{v}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277349} {:answer/id #uuid "ea08dbc3-4809-5054-a017-c2a23b383426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{8}\\,{u}^{5}{v}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277522}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277522} :answer-field/id #uuid "e911c170-af03-582c-ad47-4ba84716458b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3771f844-0efb-5152-bb73-0315652e05d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-161134" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\,v,\\,v⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{8}u.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{8}u.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =({x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2})\\,{|}_{x={u}^{2}\\,y=v\\,z=v} \\\\  & =({u}^{2}\\cdot {u}^{2})\\cdot {v}^{2}\\cdot {v}^{2}\\, \\\\  & ={u}^{4}{v}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{8}\\,{u}^{5}{v}^{4}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278395 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60908e82-b692-599f-82b9-892161866329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-{u}^{2}+3(u+v){v}^{2})\\sqrt{8{v}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276341} {:answer/id #uuid "708462c8-f5ae-5b10-8bc2-cbaa9b1a5f89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-{u}^{2}-3(u-v){v}^{2})\\sqrt{8{v}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276492} {:answer/id #uuid "7755f123-28ec-560e-a4c0-0541364afe71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "({u}^{2}-3(u+v){v}^{2})\\sqrt{8{v}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276558} {:answer/id #uuid "7f3e8ea1-6b77-5aea-ae14-47ac28753c57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-{u}^{2}-3({u}^{2}+v){v}^{2})\\sqrt{8{v}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276632} {:answer/id #uuid "a35498bf-e5d4-5d74-b5ee-fd684180144f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "({u}^{2}+3(u+v){v}^{2})\\sqrt{8{v}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276944}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276341} :answer-field/id #uuid "eebba4c6-890d-5236-a8b1-f85351eb3247" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "af99ff54-0eba-5a3e-990a-a1878cebaaed" :question/math-academy-id "q-161136" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=-{x}^{2}+3yz$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,u+v,\\,{v}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{8{v}^{2}+1}.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{8{v}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(-{x}^{2}+3yz)\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y=u+v\\,z={v}^{2}} \\\\  & =-(u\\cdot u)+3\\cdot (u+v)\\cdot ({v}^{2}) \\\\  & =-{u}^{2}+3(u+v){v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(-{u}^{2}+3(u+v){v}^{2})\\sqrt{8{v}^{2}+1}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278411 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "057c351a-27e3-59d3-8013-345a5bcfa346" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275525} {:answer/id #uuid "112a6f37-066f-50c9-88b8-4b14bca55e56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275630} {:answer/id #uuid "2a8621e4-8335-55f8-9a71-143f24ae4226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275866} {:answer/id #uuid "61176df2-a86f-5867-9df7-fe03f3d206d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276352} {:answer/id #uuid "61458edd-83a0-5587-a19b-e0a38ccdc25b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276356}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276356} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b0ae5ad-a7f1-591f-bc08-e13a68747a2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f3da3d1c-88b2-5b2c-b1c5-adc3acc48bde" :question/math-academy-id "q-161180" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}(x+2y-z)\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u-v,\\,2u+2v,\\,2v⟩\n$$\n\n \nover the domain $D={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 2}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=6.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=6.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(x+2y-z)\\,{|}_{x=u-v\\,y=2u+2v\\,z=2v} \\\\  & =(u-v)+2(2u+2v)-(2v) \\\\  & =5u+v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(5u+v)\\cdot 6\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6{∬}_{D}\\,(5u+v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{2}({\\int }_{0}^{1}(5u+v)\\,\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{5{u}^{2}}{2}+vu]}_{0}^{1}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{5}{2}+v)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6{[\\frac{5v}{2}+\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =6[5+2] \\\\  & =42.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278412 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ebe36cd-b1cf-5ce2-8a67-8db3468807b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275603} {:answer/id #uuid "18ad7ac0-5137-5083-9463-529f4f7e3804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275706} {:answer/id #uuid "4891c5fb-95c8-5c1c-a256-07260e4f9797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276121} {:answer/id #uuid "9e1ef9b7-1bf5-5710-8957-97f3d292a525" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276898} {:answer/id #uuid "c983dee7-7745-56ff-a7b7-c03e844d53b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277265}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275706} :answer-field/id #uuid "92141582-6640-51e0-ab76-f6c1c7dbae93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277984}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c6424aa2-1e21-5507-975b-b73e043b3939" :question/math-academy-id "q-161182" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}6xyz\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,u+v,\\,v⟩\n$$\n\n \nover the domain $D=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{3}.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =6xyz\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y=u+v\\,z=v} \\\\  & =6(u)(u+v)(v) \\\\  & =6({u}^{2}v+u{v}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,6({u}^{2}v+u{v}^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,({u}^{2}v+u{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}v+u{v}^{2})\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{u}^{3}v}{3}+\\frac{{u}^{2}{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{v}{3}+\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2})\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{v}^{3}}{6}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
